C = [
("h1", "6. Gabriel’s Word: Luke 1"),
("p", "Claim: the first New Testament voice on Malachi’s promise is the angel’s, and it is carefully worded. Gabriel says that a son of Zacharias will go before the Lord “in the spirit and power of Elias.” He does not say that the son will be Elias. He quotes part of Malachi 4:6 and puts the rest in other words, and he says nothing of the day or the curse. The chapter reads the whole passage in its priestly setting and then reads Zacharias’s song, so that the reader can see what the household of John believed the promise to mean."),
("sub", "6.1 A priest’s house"),
("p", "Luke opens in the days of Herod, “a certain priest named Zacharias, of the course of Abia: and his wife was of the daughters of Aaron, and her name was Elisabeth” (Luke 1:5). They were “righteous before God, walking in all the commandments and ordinances of the Lord blameless” (Luke 1:6), and had no child. The setting is the temple at the hour of incense, with “the whole multitude of the people … praying without” (Luke 1:10), and the angel stands “on the right side of the altar of incense” (Luke 1:11). It is the temple of Malachi 3:1, with a priest of the sons of Levi at its altar, and the people outside."),
("p", "The angel’s words are these: “Fear not, Zacharias: for thy prayer is heard; and thy wife Elisabeth shall bear thee a son, and thou shalt call his name John” (Luke 1:13). The son is to be “great in the sight of the Lord,” abstaining from wine and strong drink, “filled with the Holy Ghost, even from his mother’s womb” (Luke 1:15). The filling with the Spirit is said of John from the womb, and the volume reports it and leaves it there."),
("sub", "6.2 Many of the children of Israel"),
("p", "Then the work: “And many of the children of Israel shall he turn to the Lord their God” (Luke 1:16). The audience is Israel, and the number is “many.” Gabriel does not say “all,” which is the word Mark and Matthew use of the crowds who went to the Jordan, and the volume does not make the difference larger than the words allow. The turning is “to the Lord their God,” which is the direction of Elijah’s prayer on Carmel in chapter 2."),
("p", "Verse 17 follows: “And he shall go before him in the spirit and power of Elias, to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children, and the disobedient to the wisdom of the just; to make ready a people prepared for the Lord.” Four things are said. He goes “before him,” and the pronoun’s antecedent is “the Lord their God” of the verse before. He goes “in the spirit and power of Elias,” which is the form of 2 Kings 2:15, “the spirit of Elijah doth rest on Elisha.” His work is “to turn the hearts of the fathers to the children,” which is half of Malachi 4:6, with “the disobedient to the wisdom of the just” in place of the second half. And the purpose is “to make ready a people prepared for the Lord.”"),
("sub", "6.3 What Gabriel leaves out"),
("p", "Three omissions are plain. Gabriel does not say that John is Elias. He does not quote the second half of Malachi 4:6, “lest I come and smite the earth with a curse,” and he does not mention the day. And he does not say that John restores all things. The sentence is a statement of John’s office in terms of Elijah’s spirit and power, and the aim is a prepared people. A reader who hears in it the whole of Malachi 4:5, 6 hears more than the sentence says. A reader who hears in it nothing of Malachi hears less."),
("p", "The Elisha precedent of chapter 3 is the clearest guide to the form. In Kings the prophets said of Elisha, “The spirit of Elijah doth rest on Elisha” (2 Kings 2:15), and Elisha was not called Elijah. In Luke the angel says of John that he will go “in the spirit and power of Elias,” and John is not called Elias. The volume does not say that the two cases are the same. It says that the form of words is one the Old Testament had already used of a man who was not the man named, and that the form tells a reader to expect a man who bears Elijah’s spirit and power."),
("sub", "6.4 Zacharias’s song"),
("p", "When the child is born, Zacharias “was filled with the Holy Ghost, and prophesied” (Luke 1:67). The song is in Israel’s covenant terms: “Blessed be the Lord God of Israel; for he hath visited and redeemed his people, and hath raised up an horn of salvation for us in the house of his servant David; as he spake by the mouth of his holy prophets, which have been since the world began” (Luke 1:68–70). The aim is “to perform the mercy promised to our fathers, and to remember his holy covenant; the oath which he sware to our father Abraham” (Luke 1:72, 73), and the salvation is “from our enemies, and from the hand of all that hate us” (Luke 1:71)."),
("p", "Then he turns to the child: “And thou, child, shalt be called the prophet of the Highest: for thou shalt go before the face of the Lord to prepare his ways; to give knowledge of salvation unto his people by the remission of their sins, through the tender mercy of our God; whereby the dayspring from on high hath visited us” (Luke 1:76–78). The father calls the son “the prophet of the Highest.” He does not call him Elias. The child’s work is to “prepare his ways” and to “give knowledge of salvation unto his people.” The setting of the song is the house of David, the covenant with Abraham, and the people Israel, and the volume reads John in that setting."),
("ctbl", "What Luke 1 says of John and Elias.", [
    ("Verse", "What is said", "What is not said"),
    ("1:16", "Many of the children of Israel will be turned to the Lord their God", "That all will be; that the day comes"),
    ("1:17", "Goes before him in the spirit and power of Elias; turns the hearts of the fathers to the children", "That he is Elias; the curse; the day; the restoring of all things"),
    ("1:17", "To make ready a people prepared for the Lord", "That the people are made ready"),
    ("1:76", "“The prophet of the Highest,” going before the face of the Lord", "That he is called Elias"),
    ("1:68–73", "The song speaks of David, the covenant and Abraham", "Any word about the nations or a heavenly calling"),
  ], [0.14, 0.46, 0.4]),
("sub", "6.5 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Gabriel says John will go before the Lord “in the spirit and power of Elias,” a form the Old Testament used of Elisha.",
    "Luke 1:16, 17 is addressed to Israel and states a purpose, a people prepared, without reporting that the purpose was met.",
    "Gabriel quotes part of Malachi 4:6 and says nothing of the curse or the day, and does not say that John restores all things.",
    "Zacharias calls his son “the prophet of the Highest,” and the song is in the terms of David, Abraham and the covenant with Israel.",
    "Nothing in Luke 1 identifies John as Malachi’s Elijah the prophet; what it describes is a man who bears Elijah’s spirit and power.",
  ]),
]
